\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Accretive complex Gaussian integrals and cancellation}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Call #{K\in M_n(\mathbb C)} \newvocab{strictly accretive}
when its Hermitian part #{H=(K+K^*)/2} is positive definite.
For #{z_j=x_j+iy_j}, use ordinary Lebesgue volume
#{d^{2n}z=\prod_j dx_j\,dy_j}. Then #{K} is invertible,
the following integral converges absolutely, and
##{I(K):=\int_{\mathbb C^n}e^{-z^*Kz}\,d^{2n}z
       =\frac{\pi^n}{\det K}.}
Consequently, with #{Z_B(K)=\pi^{-n}I(K)} and the
[paired Grassmann value](fdip-008H)
#{Z_F(K)=J_n(e^{-\bar\psi K\psi})},
##{Z_B(K)Z_F(K)=1.}
For #{n=0} both factors are one.}

\proof{
\p{For #{n>0}, choose #{h>0} with #{H\ge hI}. Then
##{|e^{-z^*Kz}|=e^{-z^*Hz}\le e^{-h\|z\|^2}.}
The right side is integrable by the product of scalar Gaussian
integrals. If #{Kz=0}, its real quadratic value is
#{z^*Hz=0}, so #{z=0}; finite dimension makes #{K} invertible.}

\p{First suppose #{K=K^*>0}. A unitary eigenbasis gives
positive eigenvalues #{\lambda_j}. The unitary change of
variables preserves real volume, since its real Jacobian has
absolute determinant #{|\det_{\mathbb C}U|^2=1}. In each
complex coordinate the [scalar Gaussian formula](fdip-002T)
gives
##{\int_{\mathbb R^2}e^{-\lambda_j(x^2+y^2)}dx\,dy
       =\frac\pi{\lambda_j}.}
Multiplication gives #{I(K)=\pi^n/\det K}.}

\p{For general #{K}, write #{K=H+A}, where #{A^*=-A},
and set #{K(w)=H+wA} for a complex parameter #{w}.
Its Hermitian part is #{H+i(\operatorname{Im}w)A}.
Choose #{\epsilon>0} such that
#{\epsilon\|A\|<h/2}, with any positive choice if #{A=0}.
On the connected strip #{|\operatorname{Im}w|<\epsilon},
this part is at least #{hI/2}, so #{K(w)} is invertible.}

\p{The integral #{I(K(w))} is holomorphic on that strip.
Differentiation of the integrand brings down powers of
#{-z^*Az}; on every smaller neighborhood these are bounded by
a polynomial in #{\|z\|} times #{e^{-h\|z\|^2/2}}.
This integrable bound justifies differentiation under the
integral. The function #{\pi^n/\det K(w)} is holomorphic
there too. For #{w=it} with real #{|t|<\epsilon}, the matrix
#{H+itA} is Hermitian positive, so the preceding calculation
identifies the two functions. These points accumulate at the
interior point zero. The one-variable identity theorem therefore
identifies the functions throughout the strip, in particular
at #{w=1}. The Grassmann determinant then proves cancellation.}
}

\p{For Hermitian positive #{K}, the normalized density
#{(\det K/\pi^n)e^{-z^*Kz}} is a genuine Gaussian probability
law on #{2n} real coordinates. For general accretive #{K},
the weight can be complex: #{K=(1+i)} gives
#{Z_F=1+i} and #{Z_B=(1+i)^{-1}}. Normalization and determinant
cancellation still hold, but there is no positive Gaussian law
with this density. A single real Gaussian has only a determinant
power #{-1/2}; [Grassmann algebra](fdip-008F) by itself supplies
no convergence for a commuting integral.}

\p{Accretivity also survives the [Schur operation](fdip-009J).
Its principal block #{D} is accretive by testing #{K} on
#{(0,u)}, hence invertible. For #{v\ne0}, put
#{x=(v,-D^{-1}Cv)}. Then #{Kx=(Sv,0)}, and
##{\operatorname{Re}(v^*Sv)
 =\operatorname{Re}(x^*Kx)>0.}
Thus #{S} is accretive. This extra conclusion uses accretivity
of the full matrix, not just invertibility of #{D}.}

\p{\citet{Section 9.1, equations (9.3)-(9.5), p. 19}
{wingate2025lattice} describes integrating a fermion matrix
and re-expressing its determinant with commuting complex
pseudofermions. Its equation (9.5) uses #{K^{-1}} in the
commuting quadratic form, yielding a determinant rather than
its inverse when convergence holds. The cancellation here uses
#{K} in both forms and the specified factor #{\pi^{-n}};
the proof above states its convergence hypotheses explicitly.}
